// The certifying generator's fragment transcriber. Design:
// docs/certgen-transcriber-in-language-design.md §§3–4.
//
// Almost all of it is the *analysis*, and the analysis is ordinary
// program-layer Heather: inductives and `fn`s, testable and provable like any
// other library code. The layer-m part is the last block — two `match code`
// shells whose arms are calls into the helpers below: the transcriber
// (claiming `certgen.transcribe`, walking the marked body) and the signature
// checker (claiming `certgen.check_sig`, walking the fn's type as pulled by
// the reflection API — docs/fn-signature-reflection-design.md). That split is
// the artifact's point: the modal layer contributes the traversal and nothing
// else. With both shells here, the whole fragment definition — signature and
// body — reads off this one file.
//
// Elaborated on top of `lib/ir_v1.hthr`'s declarations prefix — `Ir`, `Refs`,
// `Prog`, `Val` and `import nat;`'s `add`/`sub` come from there, so this file
// declares no import and redeclares nothing.
//
// THE FIVE CONVENTIONS THIS FILE JOINS, and nothing else states them
// together:
//
// 1. MARKERS (§1). Every bound-variable occurrence in the analyzed body
//    arrives as a closed marker carrying the binder's de Bruijn LEVEL in
//    birth order: parameters `0 … arity-1` (outermost Lam first), then the
//    eliminator's field `arity`, then the induction hypothesis `arity+1`.
//    A level `≥ arity+2` is a nested eliminator's binder — outside the
//    fragment, so `TStuck`.
//
// 2. THE IR ENVIRONMENT (ir_v1 doc §3). A function body runs in the
//    environment its arguments were gathered into, slot order: parameter `i`
//    is at DISTANCE `i`, so its level in an environment of depth `d` is
//    `d - 1 - i`. A match arm sees the matched value's fields on top, so the
//    field is one deeper than the parameters.
//
// 3. CALLEE MARKERS AND CALL NUMBERING (docs/opaque-globals-analysis-
//    design.md §§3–4). A cross-function call head arrives as a closed
//    λ-chain over `Nat` ending in a numeral TAG: tag `c ≥ 1` is the `c`-th
//    distinct foreign callee, in first-marking order, and the tag→name
//    table travels beside the code (the reflection primitive's
//    `BodyCode::callees`). In the `Ir` this walker emits, callee 0 is the
//    enclosing function itself (`PSelf`, the ih marker's rendering) and a
//    foreign call renders its tag verbatim (`PCall(c, …)` → `LetCall(c,
//    …)`) — the walker's OWN numbering, mapped to names at readback; the
//    program-closure indices the certificates use are computed downstream,
//    from names, exactly as for the Rust path.
//
// 4. NON-`Nat` CONSTRUCTOR VALUES (docs/certgen-non-nat-embeddings-design.md
//    §4, widened by docs/certgen-ctor-fields-design.md). A fragment fn's
//    RESULT type may be a tier-0 enumeration whose constructors take `Nat`
//    FIELDS, so the body's constructor values arrive in the tier-0 lowering's
//    shape rather than as `Zero`/`Succ`:
//    `inr^i(inl(Pair(<nominal tag>, Pair(f₁, … Pair(f_k, Triv)))))`. The LAST
//    constructor of an n-ary sum has no trailing `inl`, and a ONE-constructor
//    enum is the bare payload with no sum. That reading mints the same tag `i`
//    the emitter's `embed_T` and the readback's own `inr^i(inl(·))` walk use,
//    so the three agree by construction rather than by comment.
//
//    The NOMINAL TAG (docs/inductive-tier0-lowering.md §3.3) is *not* a bare
//    `Triv`: it is the declaration index's binary digits as a `Pair`-spine of
//    `Triv`s, so it is only recognizable as "a value with no field in it".
//    That is what `TPure` is, and why the payload test is "the first
//    component is pure" rather than "the first component is `Triv`" — a fact
//    about the lowering that only shows up once a session declares more than
//    one inductive.
//
//    The tag and the fields are told apart by POSITION, which is the whole
//    trick: the second component of a `Pair` is always the rest of the field
//    chain, the first is a field unless it is pure, and a pure first component
//    is the nominal tag. So one state (`TFlds`) serves both "a field chain"
//    and "a payload with fields", and the walker never has to know which of
//    the two it is looking at until an `inl`/`inr` — or the fn's root — asks.
//
// 5. TIER-1 SOURCE TYPES (docs/certgen-tier1-source-types-design.md §§3–4).
//    A fn may recurse over ONE tier-1 (recursive) inductive parameter. The
//    marker pass validates the `Wf` recursor's generalization skeleton by de
//    Bruijn identity and elides it; what this walker sees is the eliminator
//    with a bare constructor CASE TREE as its step — every case scrutinizing
//    the recursion parameter's own marker — and the arm accessors as three
//    more marker shapes, told apart by λ-chain ARITY (variable identity
//    being unobservable at layer m): 2 λs over a variable = field `t` of the
//    enclosing arm (surface declaration order), 3 λs = the induction
//    hypothesis at recursive field `t`, 4 λs = the arm's field-count header.
//    In the emitted `LetMatch` the matched value's fields are prepended in
//    slot order — field `t` at distance `t` — and each arm renders at its
//    own base depth `arity + fᵢ`, which is what the header carries. The
//    `Nat` path is the degenerate instance (`Succ`'s one field recursive)
//    and keeps its own convention-1 spellings byte for byte.
//
// 6. TIER-0 PARAMETER MATCHES (docs/certgen-tier0-params-design.md §§3–4).
//    A fn may also match on a tier-0 (non-recursive, payload-free)
//    enumeration parameter. The marker pass hands the walker convention 5's
//    constructor case tree with NO eliminator around it: every scrutinee the
//    same parameter marker, every branch a header-wrapped arm (field count
//    0). A bare collected case tree at the fn's ROOT is therefore a tier-0
//    parameter match — `ta_finish` renders it as the same `LetMatch` shape,
//    dispatch slot `arity - 1 - lvl` — while a case tree anywhere else is
//    not a value and declines. There is no recursion index and no `PSelf`:
//    a tier-0 type has no structural position, so `j` is never consulted.
//
// The two meet exactly once, in `dist` and its callers: a reference is
// carried as a level all the way through composition and converted to a
// distance only when the instruction it sits in is being rendered, at that
// instruction's own environment depth. Everything else in this file is
// bookkeeping over that one sentence. A mismatch here is a non-converging
// IR — a refused certificate, never an unsound theorem (the generator is
// untrusted; issues/certifying-generator.md).

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The data.

// A reference inside a compiled prefix. `RLvl(l)` is a level in the arm's
// environment; `RLoc(j)` is "the j-th instruction of my own prefix, counted
// forward from the arm's base", which at base depth `base` is level
// `base + j`. Prefixes are concatenated and re-based during composition, and
// carrying the two kinds apart is what makes that a no-op on the references.
//
// `RFld(t)` is field `t` of the ENCLOSING ARM (surface declaration order) —
// the tier-1 marker's coordinate (convention 5). It is deferred on purpose:
// in the IR the matched value's fields are prepended in slot order, so field
// `t` sits at level `base - 1 - t` of an arm whose base depth the walker only
// learns at the eliminator (the arm's field-count header). A parameter's
// LEVEL is depth-stable and a field's DISTANCE-FROM-BASE is; each kind
// carries the stable one.
inductive PRef { RLvl(lvl: Nat), RLoc(j: Nat), RFld(t: Nat) }

inductive PRefs { QNil, QCons(r: PRef, rest: PRefs) }

// A compiled instruction prefix, stored NEWEST-INSTRUCTION-FIRST: `PCon`'s
// `rest` holds the instructions that run *before* it. Consing at the front is
// what makes `ta_succ` constant-time and index-stable.
//
// `PSelf(t)` is the structural self-call at the recursive field `t` of the
// enclosing arm (`t = 0` for the `Nat` path — `Succ`'s one field). Its
// argument list is not known here — it depends on the recursion index the
// enclosing eliminator supplies — so it is not stored; the renderer fills it
// (§4 called this a placeholder rewrite, and there is no patch pass because
// the argument list is a function of `arity`, `j`, the arm's base and `t`
// alone).
// `PCall` is a saturated foreign call, its callee carried as the marker TAG
// (convention 3) and its argument references in slot order.
inductive Pre {
    PEnd,
    PCon(tag: Nat, args: PRefs, rest: Pre),
    PSelf(t: Nat, rest: Pre),
    PCall(c: Nat, args: PRefs, rest: Pre),
}

// The walker's accumulator, one per code node visited. `TNum` is a numeral
// spine (a source literal, and a marker's payload); `TVarLeaf`/`TIdLam` exist
// only to recognize a marker; `TExp` is an expression compiled to a prefix
// plus the reference its value lands at, with `len` the prefix's length
// carried so nothing has to compute it; `TBody` is a whole function body,
// which only the eliminator produces. `TStuck` is the decline, and it
// propagates — declining is total, not partial.
// … and the two call-assembly states (convention 3): `TFn(c, rem)` is a
// callee-marker chain — `rem` lambdas around the tag numeral `c`, so `rem`
// arguments still expected — and `TPart` is a call applied to some but not
// all of them, carrying the arguments compiled so far as a merged prefix
// plus their references in slot order. Neither is a value: unapplied or
// half-applied calls decline at rendering, like every other non-value.
// … and the three NON-`Nat` CONSTRUCTOR states (convention 4): `TPure` is a
// value built from `Triv` and `Pair` alone — the NOMINAL TAG spine, and a
// field-free constructor payload, which are the same term; `TFlds` is a field
// chain (and, once its tag has been consumed, a payload WITH fields), carrying
// its fields' instructions merged into one prefix and their references in slot
// order, exactly as `TPart` carries a call's arguments; and `TCtor` is a
// payload with its constructor index, grown one `inr` at a time. All three are
// values: each renders as one `LetCon` over the fields it collected.
//
// `TFlds` and `TCtor`'s three payload fields are what the fields stage costs
// on the file's own size law — every `match` on `TA` copies each of this
// type's field types once per arm (issues/wf-field-conversion-cost.md), and
// `Pre` is the file's largest. It is ONE new constructor and no new arm in any
// existing `match`, which is the whole reason the widening is affordable
// (docs/certgen-ctor-fields-design.md §4).
// … and the TIER-1 states (convention 5, docs/certgen-tier1-source-types-
// design.md §4): `TIdLam2/3/4` are the accessor-marker heads — λ-chains over
// a variable, told apart by ARITY because variable identity is unobservable
// at layer m — reading, applied to a numeral: a field of the enclosing arm
// (2), the induction hypothesis at a recursive field (3), and an arm's
// field-count header (4). `THdr(f)` is that header awaiting its arm;
// `TArm(f, …)` an arm with its base-depth information, UNRENDERED because
// only the eliminator knows the recursion index; `TArms` the constructor
// case tree's collected arms, with the scrutinee marker's level carried so
// every node is checked to dispatch on the same parameter.
inductive Parts { PsNil, PsCons(f: Nat, pre: Pre, res: PRef, len: Nat, rest: Parts) }

inductive TA {
    TStuck,
    TNum(n: Nat),
    TVarLeaf,
    TIdLam,
    TExp(pre: Pre, res: PRef, len: Nat),
    TBody(body: Ir),
    TFn(c: Nat, rem: Nat),
    TPart(c: Nat, rem: Nat, pre: Pre, len: Nat, args: PRefs),
    TPure,
    TFlds(pre: Pre, len: Nat, args: PRefs),
    TCtor(tag: Nat, pre: Pre, len: Nat, args: PRefs),
    TIdLam2,
    TIdLam3,
    TIdLam4,
    THdr(f: Nat),
    TArm(f: Nat, pre: Pre, res: PRef, len: Nat),
    TArms(lvl: Nat, parts: Parts),
}

inductive TOut { TNo, TYes(body: Ir) }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Arithmetic. `add` and `sub` are `nat`'s; `sub` is truncated subtraction and
// curried (`sub(m)(n)` is `m - n`).

// Three-way comparison, so the marker classification below is one match
// instead of two `leb` calls whose second is only meaningful under the first.
inductive Cmp { CLt, CEq, CGt }

// Curried because both arguments descend together — the termination
// discipline's standard shape (ir_v1's `lookup`, `nat`'s `sub`).
fn nat_cmp(a: Nat) → Nat → Cmp {
    match a {
        Zero => λ (b: Nat) => match b { Zero => CEq, Succ(k) => CLt },
        Succ(a2) => λ (b: Nat) => match b { Zero => CGt, Succ(b2) => nat_cmp(a2)(b2) },
    }
}

// level `l` seen from an environment of depth `d`. Truncating subtraction
// makes this total; every call below is in range, because a level reaching
// here was minted for a binder that is live at `d`.
fn dist(d: Nat, l: Nat) → Nat { sub(sub(d)(1))(l) }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Rendering: levels to distances against the layout the instructions are
// actually emitted into. Instruction `i` of a prefix (FORWARD order) sits at
// environment depth `base + i` and its own argument references resolve at
// THAT depth; the closing `Ret` resolves at `base + len`.

fn pref_lvl(r: PRef, base: Nat) → Nat {
    match r { RLvl(l) => l, RLoc(j) => add(base, j), RFld(t) => sub(sub(base)(1))(t) }
}

fn render_refs(rs: PRefs, base: Nat, d: Nat) → Refs {
    match rs {
        QNil => RNil,
        QCons(r, rest) => RCons(dist(d, pref_lvl(r, base)), render_refs(rest, base, d)),
    }
}

// The self-call's argument list, in slot order `0 … k-1`: the parameters
// unchanged, and the recursion argument — field `t` of the enclosing arm,
// at level `base - 1 - t` — in the recursion position `j`, which is what
// makes the call structural. Slot `i` otherwise names parameter `i`, whose
// level is `k - 1 - i`. The `Nat` path is the `base = k + 1, t = 0`
// instance: the field's level is `k`, exactly as it always was.
//
// `c` counts the slots left and `i` is the slot being written, so `i` varies
// with the recursion and rides in the returned function rather than in a
// parameter (the `lookup`/`gather` idiom; ir_v1 doc §13 note 2).
fn self_arg_lvl(i: Nat, j: Nat, k: Nat, base: Nat, t: Nat) → Nat {
    match nat_cmp(i)(j) { CEq => sub(sub(base)(1))(t), _ => sub(sub(k)(1))(i) }
}

fn self_args(c: Nat, j: Nat, k: Nat, base: Nat, t: Nat, d: Nat) → Nat → Refs {
    match c {
        Zero => λ (i: Nat) => RNil,
        Succ(c2) => λ (i: Nat) =>
            RCons(dist(d, self_arg_lvl(i, j, k, base, t)), self_args(c2, j, k, base, t, d)(Succ(i))),
    }
}

// Render a prefix onto `tail`. The recursion runs newest-first — the head of
// `pre` is the LAST instruction, so it is the innermost `Let*` and `tail`
// grows outward as the depth `d` walks back down to `base`. Both of those
// vary with the recursion while `base`, `j` and `k` do not, so they are the
// returned function's arguments.
//
// Callee index 0 is `PSelf` — the enclosing function itself, by
// convention 3 — and a foreign call renders its marker tag verbatim; the
// readback maps tags to names.
fn render_pre(pre: Pre, base: Nat, j: Nat, k: Nat) → Nat → Ir → Ir {
    match pre {
        PEnd => λ (d: Nat) (tail: Ir) => tail,
        PCon(tag, args, rest) => λ (d: Nat) (tail: Ir) =>
            render_pre(rest, base, j, k)(sub(d)(1),
                LetCon(tag, render_refs(args, base, d), tail)),
        PSelf(t, rest) => λ (d: Nat) (tail: Ir) =>
            render_pre(rest, base, j, k)(sub(d)(1),
                LetCall(0, self_args(k, j, k, base, t, d)(0), tail)),
        PCall(c, args, rest) => λ (d: Nat) (tail: Ir) =>
            render_pre(rest, base, j, k)(sub(d)(1),
                LetCall(c, render_refs(args, base, d), tail)),
    }
}

// `instr_0; … ; instr_{len-1}; Ret(res)` at base depth `base`. The head
// instruction sits at `base + len - 1`; at `len = 0` that expression
// truncates, and the value is unused because the prefix is `PEnd`.
fn render_exp(pre: Pre, res: PRef, len: Nat, base: Nat, j: Nat, k: Nat) → Ir {
    render_pre(pre, base, j, k)(sub(add(base, len))(1),
        Ret(RCons(dist(add(base, len), pref_lvl(res, base)), RNil)))
}

// A numeral used as a VALUE is instructions: `LetCon(0, RNil)` for the zero,
// then one `LetCon(1, [previous])` per successor. Newest-first, so the
// successors come off the front and the zero is at the end.
fn num_pre(n: Nat) → Pre {
    match n {
        Zero => PCon(0, QNil, PEnd),
        Succ(m) => PCon(1, QCons(RLoc(m), QNil), num_pre(m)),
    }
}

// An accumulator read AS A VALUE — a prefix, the reference its value lands at,
// and the prefix's length — or the decline: a leaf, an identity lambda, an
// unapplied callee chain or a `TStuck` is not a value. `TBody` is handled by
// `ta_finish`, since it is already rendered.
//
// `VOut` is a *small* type on purpose, and it is the file's main size lever:
// every consumer of a value (`ta_call`, `render_ta`) matches this
// two-constructor type instead of matching `TA` again, and `TA` is the
// expensive one — seventeen constructors, carrying `Pre` and `Ir`, so a `match`
// on it costs Θ(arms × |lowered field type|)
// (issues/wf-field-conversion-cost.md). One `TA` match here replaces three,
// and the same rule is why no `match` on `TA` in this file contains another.
inductive VOut { VNo, VYes(pre: Pre, res: PRef, len: Nat) }

// A numeral is its instructions (`num_pre`, exactly as a numeral in any other
// value position); a compiled expression is itself; a constructor value is one
// more `LetCon` over the fields it collected, whose own value is itself — the
// same shape a saturated call renders to. A payload that never met an `inl` or
// an `inr` is a ONE-constructor enumeration's value and reads index 0, with
// its fields (`TFlds`) or without them (`TPure`) — convention 4.
fn ta_val(a: TA) → VOut {
    match a {
        TNum(n) => VYes(num_pre(n), RLoc(n), Succ(n)),
        TExp(pre, res, len) => VYes(pre, res, len),
        TCtor(tag, pre, len, args) => VYes(PCon(tag, args, pre), RLoc(len), Succ(len)),
        TFlds(pre, len, args) => VYes(PCon(0, args, pre), RLoc(len), Succ(len)),
        TPure => VYes(PCon(0, QNil, PEnd), RLoc(0), 1),
        _ => VNo,
    }
}

fn render_ta(t: TA, base: Nat, j: Nat, k: Nat) → TOut {
    match ta_val(t) {
        VYes(pre, res, len) => TYes(render_exp(pre, res, len, base, j, k)),
        VNo => TNo,
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The walker's arms (§3's `match code`). One `fn` per writable branch, plus
// the finisher; the `meta fn` shell contributes only the traversal.

fn ta_stuck() → TA { TStuck }

fn ta_zero() → TA { TNum(0) }

fn ta_var() → TA { TVarLeaf }

// A successor of a numeral is a numeral; a successor of a compiled expression
// is one more `LetCon(1, [its result])` on the front of the prefix, whose own
// value is that new instruction — local index `len`, which is why `len` is
// carried.
fn ta_succ(t: TA) → TA {
    match t {
        TNum(n) => TNum(Succ(n)),
        TExp(pre, res, len) => TExp(PCon(1, QCons(res, QNil), pre), RLoc(len), Succ(len)),
        _ => TStuck,
    }
}

// A lambda is in the fragment only as half of a marker. `d` is the domain's
// recursion result and is never consulted — a marker's domain is `Nat`,
// which the type motive does not analyze (§3 restriction 1). Fragment
// source contains no λ at all, so the recognizer is total over the two
// marker shapes: a body that is a variable leaf makes the identity (a
// variable marker's head, convention 1); a body that is a numeral — or more
// chain — grows a callee marker (convention 3).
fn ta_lam(d: TA, b: TA) → TA {
    match b {
        TVarLeaf => TIdLam,
        TNum(c) => TFn(c, 1),
        TFn(c, m) => TFn(c, Succ(m)),
        TIdLam => TIdLam2,
        TIdLam2 => TIdLam3,
        TIdLam3 => TIdLam4,
        _ => TStuck,
    }
}

// The MARKER RECOGNIZER, and the file's only place where a level is minted.
// An identity lambda applied to a numeral `i` is an occurrence of binder `i`:
// a parameter, the field, or the induction hypothesis, per convention 1.
// (Cross-function calls go through `ta_app`'s callee arms, convention 3;
// any other application is outside the fragment.)
fn ta_marker(arity: Nat, i: Nat) → TA {
    match nat_cmp(i)(arity) {
        // parameter `i`, at distance `i`, so at level `arity - 1 - i`
        CLt => TExp(PEnd, RLvl(sub(sub(arity)(1))(i)), 0),
        // the eliminator's field, born after the parameters
        CEq => TExp(PEnd, RLvl(arity), 0),
        CGt => match nat_cmp(i)(Succ(arity)) {
            // the induction hypothesis: the structural self-call, one
            // instruction whose own value is its result. `Succ`'s one field
            // is field 0 of its arm.
            CEq => TExp(PSelf(0, PEnd), RLoc(0), 1),
            _ => TStuck,
        },
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Call assembly (convention 3). The first place two compiled sub-expressions
// ever meet: each argument's prefix is appended after the ones already
// collected, so its `RLoc` indices — instruction positions counted from the
// arm's base — shift by the instructions already there. `RLvl` references
// name binders and never move.

fn shift_ref(r: PRef, off: Nat) → PRef {
    match r { RLvl(l) => RLvl(l), RLoc(j) => RLoc(add(off, j)), RFld(t) => RFld(t) }
}

fn shift_refs(rs: PRefs, off: Nat) → PRefs {
    match rs {
        QNil => QNil,
        QCons(r, rest) => QCons(shift_ref(r, off), shift_refs(rest, off)),
    }
}

fn shift_pre(p: Pre, off: Nat) → Pre {
    match p {
        PEnd => PEnd,
        PCon(tag, args, rest) => PCon(tag, shift_refs(args, off), shift_pre(rest, off)),
        PSelf(t, rest) => PSelf(t, shift_pre(rest, off)),
        PCall(c, args, rest) => PCall(c, shift_refs(args, off), shift_pre(rest, off)),
    }
}

// Newest-first lists: `a`'s instructions run AFTER `b`'s, so `a` keeps the
// front and its terminal `PEnd` becomes `b`.
fn pre_append(a: Pre, b: Pre) → Pre {
    match a {
        PEnd => b,
        PCon(tag, args, rest) => PCon(tag, args, pre_append(rest, b)),
        PSelf(t, rest) => PSelf(t, pre_append(rest, b)),
        PCall(c, args, rest) => PCall(c, args, pre_append(rest, b)),
    }
}

// Slot order is application order, so arguments append at the tail.
fn qsnoc(rs: PRefs, r: PRef) → PRefs {
    match rs {
        QNil => QCons(r, QNil),
        QCons(h, rest) => QCons(h, qsnoc(rest, r)),
    }
}

// One argument onto a call with `rem ≥ 1` slots open: merge its prefix after
// what is collected, record its reference, and either stay partial or — on
// the last slot — become the `PCall` instruction, whose own value is itself
// (local index `len`, like every emitted instruction).
fn ta_call_fin(c: Nat, rem: Nat, pre: Pre, len: Nat, args: PRefs) → TA {
    match rem {
        Zero => TExp(PCall(c, args, pre), RLoc(len), Succ(len)),
        Succ(r2) => TPart(c, rem, pre, len, args),
    }
}

fn ta_call(c: Nat, rem: Nat, pre: Pre, len: Nat, args: PRefs, a: TA) → TA {
    match ta_val(a) {
        VYes(pa, ra, la) =>
            match rem {
                Zero => TStuck,
                Succ(r2) => ta_call_fin(c, r2,
                    pre_append(shift_pre(pa, len), pre),
                    add(len, la),
                    qsnoc(args, shift_ref(ra, len))),
            },
        VNo => TStuck,
    }
}

// An application is a marker being read (convention 1) or a call being
// assembled (convention 3); everything else is outside the fragment.
// `ta_marker_at` is split out for the size reason `VOut` records: a `match` on
// `TA` nested inside another `match` on `TA` multiplies the per-arm copies of
// this type's field types, and `Pre` and `Ir` are the file's largest.
fn ta_marker_at(arity: Nat, a: TA) → TA {
    match a { TNum(i) => ta_marker(arity, i), _ => TStuck }
}

// The tier-1 accessor markers (convention 5), read by chain arity: a field
// of the enclosing arm, deferred to `RFld` (rendered once the arm's base is
// known); the ih at recursive field `t`, one `PSelf(t)` instruction; the
// arm header, holding its field count until the arm value arrives.
fn ta_fld_at(a: TA) → TA {
    match a { TNum(t) => TExp(PEnd, RFld(t), 0), _ => TStuck }
}

fn ta_ih_at(a: TA) → TA {
    match a { TNum(t) => TExp(PSelf(t, PEnd), RLoc(0), 1), _ => TStuck }
}

fn ta_hdr_at(a: TA) → TA {
    match a { TNum(f) => THdr(f), _ => TStuck }
}

fn ta_arm_of(f: Nat, v: VOut) → TA {
    match v { VYes(pre, res, len) => TArm(f, pre, res, len), VNo => TStuck }
}

fn ta_app(arity: Nat, f: TA, a: TA) → TA {
    match f {
        TIdLam => ta_marker_at(arity, a),
        TFn(c, k) => ta_call(c, k, PEnd, 0, QNil, a),
        TPart(c, r, pre, len, args) => ta_call(c, r, pre, len, args, a),
        TIdLam2 => ta_fld_at(a),
        TIdLam3 => ta_ih_at(a),
        TIdLam4 => ta_hdr_at(a),
        THdr(fc) => ta_arm_of(fc, ta_val(a)),
        _ => TStuck,
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Non-`Nat` constructor values (convention 4). A fragment fn whose RESULT type
// is a tier-0 enumeration builds its answers with the lowering's own formers,
// so the walker recognizes
// `inr^i(inl(Pair(<nominal tag>, Pair(f₁, … Pair(f_k, Triv)))))` and mints both
// the constructor index the emitter's `embed_T` uses and the fields' compiled
// instructions. Nothing here knows what the type is CALLED — that is a name,
// and names are the rim's (design §6).

// `Triv` is the leaf of every field-free value: the END of a field chain (so
// the empty chain), a digit of the nominal tag, and the whole payload of a
// nullary constructor.
fn ta_triv() → TA { TPure }

// One field onto a chain, and the only place two compiled sub-expressions meet
// outside `ta_call` — the same merge, for the same reason: the new field's
// instructions run AFTER the ones already collected, so its own `RLoc` indices
// shift by `len` while its `RLvl`s (binders) do not. Fields are collected
// right-to-left, so the new one is consed at the FRONT of the slot list.
fn ta_flds_cons(v: VOut, pre: Pre, len: Nat, args: PRefs) → TA {
    match v {
        VYes(pa, ra, la) => TFlds(pre_append(shift_pre(pa, len), pre), add(len, la),
                                  QCons(shift_ref(ra, len), args)),
        VNo => TStuck,
    }
}

// A `Pair` over the EMPTY chain (`Triv`). A pure first component is a
// nominal-tag digit or a field-free payload — the same term, deliberately not
// told apart — and anything else is the chain's last field.
fn ta_pair_end(a: TA) → TA {
    match a {
        TPure => TPure,
        _ => ta_flds_cons(ta_val(a), PEnd, 0, QNil),
    }
}

// A `Pair` over a non-empty chain. A pure first component is the payload's
// NOMINAL TAG, which is not a field and contributes nothing: the chain passes
// through unchanged and is a payload from here on. Anything else is one more
// field.
fn ta_pair_cons(a: TA, pre: Pre, len: Nat, args: PRefs) → TA {
    match a {
        TPure => TFlds(pre, len, args),
        _ => ta_flds_cons(ta_val(a), pre, len, args),
    }
}

// The second component decides which of the two a `Pair` is; the first decides
// tag-versus-field. Split into three `fn`s because the file's size rule says a
// `match` on `TA` never contains another (`VOut`'s comment): this is one
// two-arm `match` per function instead of one nested pair of them.
fn ta_pair(a: TA, b: TA) → TA {
    match b {
        TPure => ta_pair_end(a),
        TFlds(pre, len, args) => ta_pair_cons(a, pre, len, args),
        _ => TStuck,
    }
}

// `inl` wraps the payload of a non-last constructor and leaves the index at 0;
// `inr` steps past one summand, carrying the payload's collected fields
// through untouched. In the lowering `inl` never wraps anything but a payload,
// so an already-indexed value under one is outside the fragment.
fn ta_inl(t: TA) → TA {
    match t {
        TPure => TCtor(0, PEnd, 0, QNil),
        TFlds(pre, len, args) => TCtor(0, pre, len, args),
        _ => TStuck,
    }
}

fn ta_inr(t: TA) → TA {
    match t {
        TPure => TCtor(1, PEnd, 0, QNil),
        TFlds(pre, len, args) => TCtor(1, pre, len, args),
        TCtor(tag, pre, len, args) => TCtor(Succ(tag), pre, len, args),
        _ => TStuck,
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The eliminator: the one node that produces a whole body, accepted only with
// a bare parameter marker as its scrutinee (the fragment's "recursion is on
// one parameter" rule, read off the code instead of checked).

// Both arms rendered and wrapped. The zero arm runs at base depth `arity` —
// the parameters alone; the succ arm at `Succ(arity)`, the field pushed on
// top. The arm's single output is rebound as slot 0 and returned.
fn ta_elim_arms(arity: Nat, j: Nat, b: TA, s: TA) → TA {
    match render_ta(b, arity, j, arity) {
        TNo => TStuck,
        TYes(zero_ir) =>
            match render_ta(s, Succ(arity), j, arity) {
                TNo => TStuck,
                TYes(succ_ir) =>
                    TBody(LetMatch(j, Arm(zero_ir, Arm(succ_ir, NoArm)),
                        Ret(RCons(0, RNil)))),
            },
    }
}

// The scrutinee's level `l` must name a parameter; the recursion index is
// then `j = arity - 1 - l`, which is both that parameter's distance in the
// body's environment (so `LetMatch(j)`) and its slot in the self-call.
fn ta_elim_at(arity: Nat, b: TA, s: TA, l: Nat) → TA {
    match nat_cmp(l)(arity) {
        CLt => ta_elim_arms(arity, sub(sub(arity)(1))(l), b, s),
        _ => TStuck,
    }
}

fn ta_elim(arity: Nat, base: TA, step: TA, scrut: TA) → TA {
    match scrut {
        TExp(PEnd, RLvl(l), Zero) => ta_elim_at(arity, base, step, l),
        _ => TStuck,
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The TIER-1 eliminator (convention 5; docs/certgen-tier1-source-types-
// design.md §4). The marker pass hands it as: an `elim_wf` node whose step is
// the bare constructor CASE TREE — every case scrutinizing the recursion
// parameter's own marker, every branch a header-wrapped marked arm — and
// whose scrutinee is that same marker. The walker collects the arms with
// their field counts, then renders each at its own base depth
// `arity + fᵢ` and assembles the same `LetMatch` shape the `Nat` path emits,
// with one arm per constructor.

// The scrutinee-marker check, shared by the case nodes and the eliminator:
// a bare parameter occurrence, and nothing else.
inductive LOut { LNo, LYes(lvl: Nat) }

fn scrut_lvl_len(l: Nat, len: Nat) → LOut {
    match len { Zero => LYes(l), _ => LNo }
}

fn scrut_lvl_ref(res: PRef, len: Nat) → LOut {
    match res { RLvl(l) => scrut_lvl_len(l, len), _ => LNo }
}

fn scrut_lvl_pre(pre: Pre, res: PRef, len: Nat) → LOut {
    match pre { PEnd => scrut_lvl_ref(res, len), _ => LNo }
}

fn ta_scrut_lvl(sc: TA) → LOut {
    match sc { TExp(pre, res, len) => scrut_lvl_pre(pre, res, len), _ => LNo }
}

// One case node: constructor `i`'s arm on the left, the rest — the next
// case, or the last constructor's arm — on the right. Arms collect FRONT
// first, so declaration order is preserved; every node's scrutinee level is
// checked equal to the chain's.
fn ta_arms2(f: Nat, pre: Pre, res: PRef, len: Nat, f2: Nat, p2: Pre, r2: PRef, l2: Nat, sl: LOut) → TA {
    match sl {
        LYes(lv) => TArms(lv, PsCons(f, pre, res, len, PsCons(f2, p2, r2, l2, PsNil))),
        LNo => TStuck,
    }
}

fn ta_arms_join(c: Cmp, f: Nat, pre: Pre, res: PRef, len: Nat, lv: Nat, parts: Parts) → TA {
    match c { CEq => TArms(lv, PsCons(f, pre, res, len, parts)), _ => TStuck }
}

fn ta_arms_more(f: Nat, pre: Pre, res: PRef, len: Nat, lv: Nat, parts: Parts, sl: LOut) → TA {
    match sl {
        LYes(lv2) => ta_arms_join(nat_cmp(lv)(lv2), f, pre, res, len, lv, parts),
        LNo => TStuck,
    }
}

fn ta_case_r(f: Nat, pre: Pre, res: PRef, len: Nat, r: TA, sl: LOut) → TA {
    match r {
        TArm(f2, p2, r2, l2) => ta_arms2(f, pre, res, len, f2, p2, r2, l2, sl),
        TArms(lv, parts) => ta_arms_more(f, pre, res, len, lv, parts, sl),
        _ => TStuck,
    }
}

fn ta_case(l: TA, r: TA, sc: TA) → TA {
    match l {
        TArm(f, pre, res, len) => ta_case_r(f, pre, res, len, r, ta_scrut_lvl(sc)),
        _ => TStuck,
    }
}

// Render the collected arms, each at its own base depth, into the arm chain.
// Total: the field counts ride in `Parts`, so there is nothing left to
// disagree.
fn ta_wf_chain(arity: Nat, j: Nat, parts: Parts) → Ir {
    match parts {
        PsNil => NoArm,
        PsCons(f, pre, res, len, rest) =>
            Arm(render_exp(pre, res, len, add(arity, f), j, arity),
                ta_wf_chain(arity, j, rest)),
    }
}

fn ta_wf_arms(arity: Nat, j: Nat, parts: Parts) → TA {
    TBody(LetMatch(j, ta_wf_chain(arity, j, parts), Ret(RCons(0, RNil))))
}

// The recursion index off the scrutinee marker, exactly as `ta_elim` reads
// it — required equal to the level every case node dispatched on.
fn ta_wf_j(c: Cmp, arity: Nat, lv: Nat, parts: Parts) → TA {
    match c { CLt => ta_wf_arms(arity, sub(sub(arity)(1))(lv), parts), _ => TStuck }
}

fn ta_wf_lv(c: Cmp, arity: Nat, lv: Nat, parts: Parts) → TA {
    match c { CEq => ta_wf_j(nat_cmp(lv)(arity), arity, lv, parts), _ => TStuck }
}

fn ta_wf_at(arity: Nat, lv: Nat, parts: Parts, sl: LOut) → TA {
    match sl { LYes(lv2) => ta_wf_lv(nat_cmp(lv)(lv2), arity, lv, parts), LNo => TStuck }
}

fn ta_elim_wf(arity: Nat, step: TA, sc: TA) → TA {
    match step { TArms(lv, parts) => ta_wf_at(arity, lv, parts, ta_scrut_lvl(sc)), _ => TStuck }
}

// A TIER-0 parameter match (convention 6): the collected case tree, arrived
// at the fn's root with no eliminator around it. The dispatch level must
// name a parameter — the recursion-index conversion `ta_elim_at` makes,
// reused as the dispatch slot — and each arm renders at its own base depth
// through the tier-1 chain renderer, every field count 0 in practice.
// `j` is passed as the dispatch slot only because `render_exp` wants one;
// no `PSelf` can occur here (a self-call on a tier-0 parameter is not
// structural, and the surface refuses the recursion before this walker
// ever sees it — a `PSelf` smuggled in anyway renders a `LetCall(0, …)`
// whose certificate the fold declines at mask 0).
fn ta_arms_at(arity: Nat, j: Nat, parts: Parts) → TOut {
    TYes(LetMatch(j, ta_wf_chain(arity, j, parts), Ret(RCons(0, RNil))))
}

fn ta_arms_finish(c: Cmp, arity: Nat, lv: Nat, parts: Parts) → TOut {
    match c { CLt => ta_arms_at(arity, sub(sub(arity)(1))(lv), parts), _ => TNo }
}

// The root. A body that is already an eliminator is done; a bare case tree
// is a tier-0 parameter match (convention 6); a body that is an
// expression is a non-recursive fragment fn (`fn seven() → Nat { 7 }`) and
// renders at base depth `arity` with no recursion index to speak of — `j` is
// unused there because no `PSelf` can occur without the eliminator that mints
// its marker.
fn ta_finish(arity: Nat, t: TA) → TOut {
    match t {
        TBody(body) => TYes(body),
        TArms(lv, parts) => ta_arms_finish(nat_cmp(lv)(arity), arity, lv, parts),
        _ => render_ta(t, arity, 0, arity),
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The signature checker (docs/fn-signature-reflection-design.md §2). The
// analyzed fn's TYPE arrives as code — pulled by the elaborator's reflection
// primitive, `TyCode(g)` in the shell below — and the fragment signature rule
// lives here, whole: at least one parameter, each a `Nat` or a payload-free
// tier-0 enumeration, at most one of them a tier-1 inductive, `R` a `Nat` or
// a tier-0 enumeration. `SigNo` is the decline, total like `TStuck`.

// The walk's accumulator, one state per shape the fragment signature is made
// of. `SNat` is the one leaf that is a `Nat`; `SPure` is anything built from
// `Unit` and Σ alone — which covers `Unit` itself, the NOMINAL TAG type
// (`docs/inductive-tier0-lowering.md` §3.3: the declaration index's binary
// digits, so not a bare `Unit` in general), and a whole field-free payload
// `Σ(<tag>). Unit`. `SFlds` is a Σ chain of `Nat`s — a constructor's FIELD
// spine, and (once its tag is consumed) a payload with fields; it mirrors the
// term side's `TFlds` exactly, position for position. `SEnum(k)` is a
// right-nested sum of `k` payloads — the lowering of a tier-0 enumeration with
// `k` constructors; `SFn` is a fragment signature. `SNo` is the decline, total
// like `TStuck`.
// … and `SWf`: a recognized TIER-1 parameter type — a `Wf` over an
// enumeration-shaped payload (the shape is tier 0's lowering with the
// recursive fields deleted, so the existing states read it verbatim). The
// arity term is deliberately unread: which fields are recursive is a
// declaration fact the rim owns, and its walk would drag term arms into a
// type walk for nothing the verdict states
// (docs/certgen-tier1-source-types-design.md §5).
inductive SA {
    SNo,
    SNat,
    SPure,
    SEnum(k: Nat),
    SFlds,
    SWf,
    SFn(arity: Nat, res: Nat, wf: Nat),
}

// The verdict. `res` is a single `Nat`: 0 means the result type is `Nat`,
// `k >= 1` means it is an enumeration with `k` constructors — which is all the
// rim needs, since the constructors' NAMES are a declaration fact it already
// has (design doc §§5–6). Flat on purpose: every field here is a `Nat`, so no
// elimination over this type pays for a lowered inductive field
// (issues/wf-field-conversion-cost.md).
// `wf` is a single `Nat` too: 0 means every parameter is a `Nat`;
// `Succ(d)` means the (one) tier-1 parameter sits `d` slots from the END of
// the parameter list — suffix-counted because the walk folds from the right,
// so a `Nat` domain leaves it unchanged and the rim converts once.
inductive SigOut { SigNo, SigYes(arity: Nat, res: Nat, wf: Nat) }

// A Σ over `Unit` — the end of a field spine. Two pures are pure again (a tag
// spine, a field-free payload); a `Nat` there is the last field of a
// one-field constructor.
//
// A field whose type is another INDUCTIVE declines here, and that is this
// stage's boundary rather than an accident: the term side would read such a
// field perfectly well (it is a value like any other), but `embed_T` would
// have to embed it at the field type's own embedding, which is emitter work
// (docs/certgen-ctor-fields-design.md §6).
fn sig_sigma_end(fst: SA) → SA {
    match fst {
        SPure => SPure,
        SNat => SFlds,
        _ => SNo,
    }
}

// A Σ over a field spine. `SPure` in the first component is the payload's
// NOMINAL TAG — consumed, not counted — and `SNat` is one more field; both
// answer `SFlds`, exactly as `ta_pair_cons` answers `TFlds` for both.
fn sig_sigma_cons(fst: SA) → SA {
    match fst {
        SPure => SFlds,
        SNat => SFlds,
        _ => SNo,
    }
}

fn sig_sigma(fst: SA, snd: SA) → SA {
    match snd {
        SPure => sig_sigma_end(fst),
        SFlds => sig_sigma_cons(fst),
        _ => SNo,
    }
}

// A sum is right-nested and only its LAST summand is a bare payload, so the
// left side is always one constructor — with fields (`SFlds`) or without
// (`SPure`) — and the right side is either the last one or the rest of the
// enumeration.
fn sig_sum_pay(rhs: SA) → SA {
    match rhs {
        SPure => SEnum(2),
        SFlds => SEnum(2),
        SEnum(k) => SEnum(Succ(k)),
        _ => SNo,
    }
}

fn sig_sum(lhs: SA, rhs: SA) → SA {
    match lhs {
        SPure => sig_sum_pay(rhs),
        SFlds => sig_sum_pay(rhs),
        _ => SNo,
    }
}

// A Π is in the fragment iff its domain is a bare `Nat` — a higher-order
// `Nat → Nat` domain arrives as `SFn(1, 0)` and declines — and its codomain
// is either the result type or a fragment signature one parameter shorter.
// A one-constructor enumeration has no sum at all, so a bare payload is a
// result too — with fields (`SFlds`) or without (`SPure`).
fn sig_pi_nat(cod: SA) → SA {
    match cod {
        SNat => SFn(1, 0, 0),
        SPure => SFn(1, 1, 0),
        SFlds => SFn(1, 1, 0),
        SEnum(k) => SFn(1, k, 0),
        SFn(a, r, w) => SFn(Succ(a), r, w),
        _ => SNo,
    }
}

// A tier-1 domain: the fragment admits ONE, so a codomain that already
// carries one declines. `SWf` in a codomain position is not a result state,
// which is where "a tier-1 result type declines" still lives.
fn sig_pi_wf_fn(a: Nat, r: Nat, w: Nat) → SA {
    match w { Zero => SFn(Succ(a), r, Succ(a)), Succ(k) => SNo }
}

fn sig_pi_wf(cod: SA) → SA {
    match cod {
        SNat => SFn(1, 0, Succ(0)),
        SPure => SFn(1, 1, Succ(0)),
        SFlds => SFn(1, 1, Succ(0)),
        SEnum(k) => SFn(1, k, Succ(0)),
        SFn(a, r, w) => sig_pi_wf_fn(a, r, w),
        _ => SNo,
    }
}

// A TIER-0 enumeration domain (docs/certgen-tier0-params-design.md §5): a
// one-constructor payload (`SPure`) or a right-nested sum (`SEnum`) is a
// parameter like a `Nat` — arity up one, the tier-1 position untouched — so
// the continuation is `sig_pi_nat`'s. `SFlds` as a domain still declines (a
// fielded one-constructor payload); a fielded summand inside an `SEnum` is
// invisible to this walk by design, and the rim's field rule declines the
// declaration — the checker-accepts/rim-declines split, not a drift.
fn sig_pi(dom: SA, cod: SA) → SA {
    match dom {
        SNat => sig_pi_nat(cod),
        SPure => sig_pi_nat(cod),
        SEnum(k) => sig_pi_nat(cod),
        SWf => sig_pi_wf(cod),
        _ => SNo,
    }
}

// A `Wf` whose shape reads as an enumeration — with or without `Nat`
// fields — is a recognized tier-1 parameter type. Which DECLARATION it is,
// and whether that declaration is nameable, is the rim's question, exactly
// as for a monomorphized enumeration result.
fn sig_wf(shape: SA) → SA {
    match shape {
        SPure => SWf,
        SFlds => SWf,
        SEnum(k) => SWf,
        _ => SNo,
    }
}

// The whole-signature rule: only a `SFn` is a signature at all, and arity 0
// cannot arise from `sig_pi` — a bare `Nat` or a bare enumeration is a nullary
// fn, which has no calling convention to certify, so it declines here.
fn sig_finish(s: SA) → SigOut {
    match s {
        SFn(a, r, w) => match a { Zero => SigNo, Succ(k) => SigYes(Succ(k), r, w) },
        _ => SigNo,
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Computation checks. These are the cross-check discipline (§5) run by
// conversion instead of by `conv_term` from Rust: each right-hand side is
// what `crates/elab/src/certgen.rs`'s `Compiler` emits for the same function.
// A divergence is a failed `refl`, here, at library elaboration time.

// `fn seven() → Nat { 7 }`'s shape at 2: a numeral body is instructions.
theorem ta_numeral_body():
    ta_finish(1, ta_succ(ta_succ(ta_zero())))
    = TYes(LetCon(0, RNil,
          LetCon(1, RCons(0, RNil),
          LetCon(1, RCons(0, RNil),
          Ret(RCons(0, RNil))))))
{ refl }

theorem ta_zero_body():
    ta_finish(0, ta_zero()) = TYes(LetCon(0, RNil, Ret(RCons(0, RNil))))
{ refl }

// `fn dbl(n: Nat) → Nat { match n { Zero => Zero, Succ(k) => Succ(Succ(dbl(k))) } }`
// — the markers spelled as the walker builds them: `ta_lam(_, ta_var())` is
// the identity, its argument the numeral from the `zero`/`succ` arms. Level 0
// is the parameter, level 2 = arity+1 the induction hypothesis.
theorem dbl_transcribes():
    ta_elim(1,
        ta_zero(),
        ta_succ(ta_succ(ta_app(1, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()),
                               ta_succ(ta_succ(ta_zero()))))),
        ta_app(1, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_zero()))
    = TBody(LetMatch(0,
          Arm(LetCon(0, RNil, Ret(RCons(0, RNil))),
          Arm(LetCall(0, RCons(0, RNil),
              LetCon(1, RCons(0, RNil),
              LetCon(1, RCons(0, RNil),
              Ret(RCons(0, RNil))))),
          NoArm)),
          Ret(RCons(0, RNil))))
{ refl }

// `fn add(a, b: Nat) → Nat { match b { Zero => a, Succ(k) => Succ(add(a, k)) } }`
// — arity 2, recursion on the SECOND parameter, so `j` is 1 and the self-call
// carries `a` unchanged in slot 0 and the field in slot 1. This is the
// generator's own `add` emission, `crates/elab/tests/certgen.rs`'s
// `generated_add_matches_the_hand_run` verbatim.
theorem add_transcribes():
    ta_elim(2,
        ta_app(2, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_zero()),
        ta_succ(ta_app(2, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()),
                       ta_succ(ta_succ(ta_succ(ta_zero()))))),
        ta_app(2, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_succ(ta_zero())))
    = TBody(LetMatch(1,
          Arm(Ret(RCons(0, RNil)),
          Arm(LetCall(0, RCons(1, RCons(0, RNil)),
              LetCon(1, RCons(0, RNil),
              Ret(RCons(0, RNil)))),
          NoArm)),
          Ret(RCons(0, RNil))))
{ refl }

// `fn viaadd(a, b: Nat) → Nat { match b { Zero => Zero, Succ(k) => add(a, viaadd(a, k)) } }`
// — the cross-function positive (docs/opaque-globals-analysis-design.md §4).
// The fixture was named `mul` until 2026-08-13, when `std/nat` took that name
// for a library function with a different body
// (issues/std-nat-multiplication.md); this theorem is unchanged but for its
// own name, since it pins markers and never mentions the fn.
// The foreign head arrives as the callee chain `λλ.⌜1⌝`
// (`ta_lam(_, ta_lam(_, 1))`), applied to parameter `a` (marker level 0) and
// to the self-call, which is the ih marker (level `arity+1 = 3`, compiling
// to the one-`PSelf` prefix). In the walker's own numbering the succ arm is
// self first (`LetCall(0, …)` — the recursion's result), then the foreign
// call by its tag (`LetCall(1, …)` reading `a` at distance 2 and the
// self-call's value at 0). This is `crates/elab/tests/certgen.rs`'s Rust v2
// `viaadd` emission verbatim after the readback maps 0/1 to `viaadd`/`add`
// and the closure renumbers.
theorem viaadd_transcribes():
    ta_elim(2,
        ta_zero(),
        ta_app(2,
            ta_app(2,
                ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_succ(ta_zero()))),
                ta_app(2, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_zero())),
            ta_app(2, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()),
                   ta_succ(ta_succ(ta_succ(ta_zero()))))),
        ta_app(2, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_succ(ta_zero())))
    = TBody(LetMatch(1,
          Arm(LetCon(0, RNil, Ret(RCons(0, RNil))),
          Arm(LetCall(0, RCons(1, RCons(0, RNil)),
              LetCall(1, RCons(2, RCons(0, RNil)),
              Ret(RCons(0, RNil)))),
          NoArm)),
          Ret(RCons(0, RNil))))
{ refl }

// A half-applied call is not a value: the chain took one argument of two.
theorem call_partial_declines():
    ta_finish(2,
        ta_app(2, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_succ(ta_zero()))),
               ta_zero()))
    = TNo
{ refl }

// …and an unapplied callee chain is not a value either.
theorem callee_chain_declines():
    ta_finish(1, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_succ(ta_zero()))) = TNo
{ refl }

// The decline: a marker at `arity + 2` is a nested eliminator's binder, which
// the fragment does not contain.
theorem nested_binder_sticks():
    ta_app(1, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()),
           ta_succ(ta_succ(ta_succ(ta_zero()))))
    = TStuck
{ refl }

theorem nested_binder_declines():
    ta_finish(1, ta_app(1, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()),
                        ta_succ(ta_succ(ta_succ(ta_zero())))))
    = TNo
{ refl }

// …and the eliminator refuses a scrutinee that is not a bare parameter: a
// numeral is not a variable occurrence.
theorem elim_needs_a_parameter():
    ta_elim(1, ta_zero(), ta_zero(), ta_zero()) = TStuck
{ refl }

// Non-`Nat` constructor values (convention 4), spelled as the walker builds
// them. A two-constructor enumeration's SECOND constructor is `inr` of the
// payload `Pair(<tag>, Triv)`, and it renders as one instruction at tag 1.
theorem ctor_second_of_two():
    ta_finish(1, ta_inr(ta_pair(ta_triv(), ta_triv())))
    = TYes(LetCon(1, RNil, Ret(RCons(0, RNil))))
{ refl }

// …the FIRST is `inl` of the same payload, and reads tag 0 — which is also
// what a one-constructor enumeration's bare payload reads.
theorem ctor_first_of_two():
    ta_finish(1, ta_inl(ta_pair(ta_triv(), ta_triv())))
    = TYes(LetCon(0, RNil, Ret(RCons(0, RNil))))
{ refl }

theorem ctor_of_one():
    ta_finish(1, ta_pair(ta_triv(), ta_triv()))
    = TYes(LetCon(0, RNil, Ret(RCons(0, RNil))))
{ refl }

// …and the THIRD of three is `inr(inr(payload))`, a tag no `Nat` body can
// produce.
theorem ctor_third_of_three():
    ta_finish(1, ta_inr(ta_inr(ta_pair(ta_triv(), ta_triv()))))
    = TYes(LetCon(2, RNil, Ret(RCons(0, RNil))))
{ refl }

// …and the nominal tag is a `Pair`-spine of `Triv`s, not a bare `Triv`, so the
// same constructor at a later declaration index reads exactly the same.
theorem ctor_at_a_nonzero_nominal_tag():
    ta_finish(1, ta_inr(ta_pair(ta_pair(ta_pair(ta_triv(), ta_triv()), ta_triv()),
                                ta_triv())))
    = TYes(LetCon(1, RNil, Ret(RCons(0, RNil))))
{ refl }

// `inl` wraps a payload and nothing else: in the lowering it never sits over
// an already-indexed value.
theorem inl_of_an_index_declines():
    ta_inl(ta_inr(ta_pair(ta_triv(), ta_triv()))) = TStuck
{ refl }

// CONSTRUCTOR FIELDS (docs/certgen-ctor-fields-design.md). A payload with a
// value in it is a constructor with a field: the field is compiled like any
// other expression and its reference becomes an argument of the one `LetCon`.
// `PSome(0)` of `Peek {PNone, PSome(k: Nat)}` — the literal is its own
// instruction, and the constructor reads it at distance 0.
theorem ctor_with_one_field():
    ta_finish(1, ta_inr(ta_pair(ta_triv(), ta_pair(ta_zero(), ta_triv()))))
    = TYes(LetCon(0, RNil, LetCon(1, RCons(0, RNil), Ret(RCons(0, RNil)))))
{ refl }

// TWO fields, at two parameters of a 2-ary fn: slot order is the chain's
// order, left to right, which is what the emitter's `embed_T` and `ctor_spec`
// both assume. Parameter 0 is level 1 in a depth-2 environment (distance 0)
// and parameter 1 is level 0 (distance 1), so a swapped field list is visible
// here as `RCons(1, RCons(0, …))`.
theorem ctor_field_order():
    ta_finish(2, ta_inl(ta_pair(ta_triv(),
        ta_pair(ta_app(2, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_zero()),
        ta_pair(ta_app(2, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_succ(ta_zero())),
                ta_triv())))))
    = TYes(LetCon(0, RCons(0, RCons(1, RNil)), Ret(RCons(0, RNil))))
{ refl }

// A field is an arbitrary expression, so it carries its own INSTRUCTIONS and
// they are merged before the constructor's: the numeral 2 is three `LetCon`s,
// and the third constructor of a three-constructor enumeration reads their
// result at distance 0.
theorem ctor_field_is_computed():
    ta_finish(1, ta_inr(ta_inr(ta_pair(ta_triv(),
        ta_pair(ta_succ(ta_succ(ta_zero())), ta_triv())))))
    = TYes(LetCon(0, RNil,
          LetCon(1, RCons(0, RNil),
          LetCon(1, RCons(0, RNil),
          LetCon(2, RCons(0, RNil), Ret(RCons(0, RNil)))))))
{ refl }

// …and the merge is the one `ta_call` already does, so a field may be a
// CROSS-FUNCTION CALL: the callee marker's tag rides through to the `LetCall`
// and the constructor reads its result. This is what makes cross-function
// composition at a non-`Nat` result land with fields rather than after them
// (docs/certgen-ctor-fields-design.md §5).
theorem ctor_field_is_a_call():
    ta_finish(1, ta_inr(ta_pair(ta_triv(),
        ta_pair(ta_app(1, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_succ(ta_zero())),
                       ta_app(1, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_zero())),
                ta_triv()))))
    = TYes(LetCall(1, RCons(0, RNil),
          LetCon(1, RCons(0, RNil), Ret(RCons(0, RNil)))))
{ refl }

// A field that is not a VALUE — a bare variable leaf, an unapplied callee
// chain — is not a constructor argument the walker can compile, and declining
// is total: the whole payload sticks.
theorem ctor_field_must_be_a_value_declines():
    ta_pair(ta_var(), ta_triv()) = TStuck
{ refl }

// TIER-1 SOURCE TYPES (convention 5). The accessor markers first, read by
// λ-chain arity: two λs over a variable applied to a numeral is field `t` of
// the enclosing arm — deferred as `RFld`, whose distance is `t` at any
// depth…
theorem tier1_field_marker():
    ta_app(1, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var())), ta_zero())
    = TExp(PEnd, RFld(0), 0)
{ refl }

// …three λs is the induction hypothesis at recursive field `t`, one
// `PSelf(t)` instruction whose own value is its result…
theorem tier1_ih_marker():
    ta_app(1, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()))),
           ta_succ(ta_zero()))
    = TExp(PSelf(1, PEnd), RLoc(0), 1)
{ refl }

// …and four λs is an arm's field-count header, holding `f` until the arm
// value arrives.
theorem tier1_arm_header():
    ta_app(1,
        ta_app(1,
            ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var())))),
            ta_succ(ta_succ(ta_zero()))),
        ta_zero())
    = TArm(2, PCon(0, QNil, PEnd), RLoc(0), 1)
{ refl }

// The whole linear transcription, spelled as the walker builds it: a
// two-constructor tier-1 type (`Toks { TNil, TCons(n: Nat, rest: Toks) }`),
// a 1-ary fn recursing on it (`tsum`), the `TNil` arm a numeral and the
// `TCons` arm `Succ(ih@field 1)`. The rendered arms sit at their own base
// depths (`arity + 0`, `arity + 2`): the self-call gathers the child at
// distance 1 — field 1 of the arm — and the emitted `LetMatch` is
// byte-identical to the round's hand-run
// (docs/certgen-tier1-source-types-design.md §1).
theorem tier1_linear_transcribes():
    ta_elim_wf(1,
        ta_case(
            ta_app(1,
                ta_app(1,
                    ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var())))),
                    ta_zero()),
                ta_zero()),
            ta_app(1,
                ta_app(1,
                    ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var())))),
                    ta_succ(ta_succ(ta_zero()))),
                ta_succ(ta_app(1,
                    ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()))),
                    ta_succ(ta_zero())))),
            ta_app(1, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_zero())),
        ta_app(1, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_zero()))
    = TBody(LetMatch(0,
          Arm(LetCon(0, RNil, Ret(RCons(0, RNil))),
          Arm(LetCall(0, RCons(1, RNil),
              LetCon(1, RCons(0, RNil),
              Ret(RCons(0, RNil)))),
          NoArm)),
          Ret(RCons(0, RNil))))
{ refl }

// A payload field used as the arm's value renders at its own distance —
// field 0 at distance 0 whatever the arm's depth (`RFld` carries the
// distance-from-base, the depth-stable coordinate for a prepended field).
theorem tier1_payload_arm_renders():
    ta_elim_wf(1,
        ta_case(
            ta_app(1,
                ta_app(1,
                    ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var())))),
                    ta_succ(ta_zero())),
                ta_app(1, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var())), ta_zero())),
            ta_app(1,
                ta_app(1,
                    ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var())))),
                    ta_succ(ta_succ(ta_zero()))),
                ta_succ(ta_app(1,
                    ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()))),
                    ta_zero()))),
            ta_app(1, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_zero())),
        ta_app(1, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_zero()))
    = TBody(LetMatch(0,
          Arm(Ret(RCons(0, RNil)),
          Arm(LetCall(0, RCons(0, RNil),
              LetCon(1, RCons(0, RNil),
              Ret(RCons(0, RNil)))),
          NoArm)),
          Ret(RCons(0, RNil))))
{ refl }

// A case node whose scrutinee is not the same parameter as the chain's
// declines: the dispatch tree is about ONE recursion parameter.
theorem tier1_mixed_scrutinee_declines():
    ta_elim_wf(2,
        ta_case(
            ta_app(2,
                ta_app(2,
                    ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var())))),
                    ta_zero()),
                ta_zero()),
            ta_app(2,
                ta_app(2,
                    ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var())))),
                    ta_zero()),
                ta_zero()),
            ta_app(2, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_zero())),
        ta_app(2, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_succ(ta_zero())))
    = TStuck
{ refl }

// An arm that never got its header is not an arm.
theorem tier1_headerless_arm_declines():
    ta_case(ta_zero(), ta_zero(), ta_app(1, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_zero()))
    = TStuck
{ refl }

// TIER-0 PARAMETER MATCHES (convention 6). The case tree with no eliminator
// around it, at the fn's root, is a match on a tier-0 enumeration parameter.
// `pick(f: Flag, a, b) { match f { Off => a, On => b } }`, spelled as the
// walker builds it: two header-wrapped arms (field count 0) dispatching on
// parameter 0 (marker level 0, walker level 2), each arm a bare parameter
// reference. The emitted `LetMatch(0, …)` is byte-identical to the round's
// hand-run (docs/certgen-tier0-params-design.md §1).
theorem t0_case_transcribes():
    ta_finish(3,
        ta_case(
            ta_app(3,
                ta_app(3,
                    ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var())))),
                    ta_zero()),
                ta_app(3, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_succ(ta_zero()))),
            ta_app(3,
                ta_app(3,
                    ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var())))),
                    ta_zero()),
                ta_app(3, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_succ(ta_succ(ta_zero())))),
            ta_app(3, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_zero())))
    = TYes(LetMatch(0,
          Arm(Ret(RCons(1, RNil)),
          Arm(Ret(RCons(2, RNil)),
          NoArm)),
          Ret(RCons(0, RNil))))
{ refl }

// …the dispatch level must name a PARAMETER: a tree dispatching on the
// eliminator-field level (`arity`) is not a tier-0 parameter match.
theorem t0_case_on_a_nonparameter_declines():
    ta_finish(1,
        ta_case(
            ta_app(1,
                ta_app(1,
                    ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var())))),
                    ta_zero()),
                ta_zero()),
            ta_app(1,
                ta_app(1,
                    ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var())))),
                    ta_zero()),
                ta_zero()),
            ta_app(1, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_succ(ta_zero()))))
    = TNo
{ refl }

// …and a case tree in a VALUE position is not a value: only the root may
// match.
theorem t0_case_in_value_position_declines():
    ta_succ(
        ta_case(
            ta_app(1,
                ta_app(1,
                    ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var())))),
                    ta_zero()),
                ta_zero()),
            ta_app(1,
                ta_app(1,
                    ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var())))),
                    ta_zero()),
                ta_zero()),
            ta_app(1, ta_lam(ta_stuck(), ta_var()), ta_zero())))
    = TStuck
{ refl }

// The signature checker's own pins: `dbl`'s and `add`'s signatures, spelled
// as the walker builds them (`SNat` is the `ty_nat` arm's answer), and the
// declines the fragment rule turns on.
theorem sig_dbl(): sig_finish(sig_pi(SNat, SNat)) = SigYes(1, 0, 0) { refl }

theorem sig_add():
    sig_finish(sig_pi(SNat, sig_pi(SNat, SNat))) = SigYes(2, 0, 0)
{ refl }

// A bare `Nat` is a nullary fn: no calling convention to certify.
theorem sig_nullary_declines(): sig_finish(SNat) = SigNo { refl }

// A higher-order domain — `(Nat → Nat) → Nat` — is outside the fragment.
theorem sig_higher_order_declines():
    sig_pi(sig_pi(SNat, SNat), SNat) = SNo
{ refl }

// A NOMINAL TAG type is a Σ-spine of `Unit`s and `Σ(Unit).Unit` digits, so it
// reads `SPure` — and so does a payload with no fields, which is why the two
// are deliberately not told apart.
theorem sig_tag_is_pure():
    sig_sigma(sig_sigma(SPure, SPure), SPure) = SPure
{ refl }

// `Nat → Flag`, two nullary constructors: the verdict carries the constructor
// COUNT beside the arity (docs/certgen-non-nat-embeddings-design.md §5).
theorem sig_nat_to_two_ctors():
    sig_finish(sig_pi(SNat, sig_sum(SPure, SPure))) = SigYes(1, 2, 0)
{ refl }

// A three-constructor enumeration is right-nested, and its count comes off the
// nesting rather than off anything the rim supplies.
theorem sig_three_ctors():
    sig_sum(SPure, sig_sum(SPure, SPure)) = SEnum(3)
{ refl }

// A constructor FIELD — a `Nat` inside the payload's Σ chain — reads `SFlds`,
// the type-side mirror of `TFlds`: the tag is consumed and the fields are what
// is left (docs/certgen-ctor-fields-design.md §4).
theorem sig_ctor_with_a_field():
    sig_sigma(SPure, sig_sigma(SNat, SPure)) = SFlds
{ refl }

// …two fields read the same state, because the verdict counts CONSTRUCTORS and
// the rim already knows each one's field count from the declaration (§5).
theorem sig_ctor_with_two_fields():
    sig_sigma(SPure, sig_sigma(SNat, sig_sigma(SNat, SPure))) = SFlds
{ refl }

// …so a declaration one of whose constructors carries a field is in whole, and
// still counts two constructors.
theorem sig_enum_with_a_field():
    sig_finish(sig_pi(SNat,
        sig_sum(SPure, sig_sigma(SPure, sig_sigma(SNat, SPure)))))
    = SigYes(1, 2, 0)
{ refl }

// A field whose type is another INDUCTIVE is out: the term side would read it,
// but `embed_T` would have to embed it at that type's own embedding, which is
// emitter work this stage does not do (docs/certgen-ctor-fields-design.md §6).
theorem sig_ctor_with_an_enum_field_declines():
    sig_sigma(SPure, sig_sigma(sig_sum(SPure, SPure), SPure)) = SNo
{ refl }

// A ONE-constructor enumeration has no sum at all, and is still a result.
theorem sig_one_ctor_result():
    sig_finish(sig_pi(SNat, SPure)) = SigYes(1, 1, 0)
{ refl }

theorem sig_unrecognized_result_declines():
    sig_finish(sig_pi(SNat, SNo)) = SigNo
{ refl }

// A tier-1 parameter: a `Wf` over an enumeration-shaped payload reads `SWf`,
// and the verdict carries its suffix position beside the arity
// (docs/certgen-tier1-source-types-design.md §5). `Toks → Nat`…
theorem sig_tier1_param():
    sig_finish(sig_pi(sig_wf(sig_sum(SPure, sig_sigma(SPure, sig_sigma(SNat, SPure)))), SNat))
    = SigYes(1, 0, Succ(0))
{ refl }

// …`Th → Nat → Nat` — the tier-1 parameter two slots from the end.
theorem sig_tier1_param_first_of_two():
    sig_finish(sig_pi(sig_wf(sig_sum(SPure, SFlds)), sig_pi(SNat, SNat)))
    = SigYes(2, 0, Succ(1))
{ refl }

// Two tier-1 parameters decline: `SigOut` carries one position because the
// fragment admits one.
theorem sig_two_tier1_params_decline():
    sig_pi(SWf, SFn(1, 0, Succ(0))) = SNo
{ refl }

// A tier-1 RESULT still declines — `SWf` is not a result state.
theorem sig_tier1_result_declines():
    sig_pi(SNat, SWf) = SNo
{ refl }

// A `Wf` whose shape is not an enumeration's is not a fragment parameter.
theorem sig_wf_junk_shape_declines():
    sig_wf(SNat) = SNo
{ refl }

// A TIER-0 enumeration parameter (docs/certgen-tier0-params-design.md §5):
// `Flag → Nat`…
theorem sig_t0_param():
    sig_finish(sig_pi(sig_sum(SPure, SPure), SNat)) = SigYes(1, 0, 0)
{ refl }

// …`Flag → Nat → Nat` — a tier-0 parameter beside a `Nat` one…
theorem sig_t0_param_beside_nat():
    sig_finish(sig_pi(sig_sum(SPure, SPure), sig_pi(SNat, SNat))) = SigYes(2, 0, 0)
{ refl }

// …TWO tier-0 parameters, unlike the one-tier-1 rule: no position is
// carried, so no state limits the count…
theorem sig_t0_params_two():
    sig_finish(sig_pi(sig_sum(SPure, SPure),
        sig_pi(sig_sum(SPure, sig_sum(SPure, SPure)), SNat)))
    = SigYes(2, 0, 0)
{ refl }

// …`Flag → Flag` — the tier-0 parameter and the tier-0 RESULT composed…
theorem sig_t0_param_enum_result():
    sig_finish(sig_pi(sig_sum(SPure, SPure), sig_sum(SPure, SPure)))
    = SigYes(1, 2, 0)
{ refl }

// …`Toks → Flag → Nat` — a tier-0 parameter beside the tier-1 one, the
// `wf` position untouched by the tier-0 domain.
theorem sig_t0_beside_tier1():
    sig_finish(sig_pi(sig_wf(sig_sum(SPure, sig_sigma(SPure, sig_sigma(SNat, SPure)))),
        sig_pi(sig_sum(SPure, SPure), SNat)))
    = SigYes(2, 0, Succ(1))
{ refl }

// A fielded ONE-constructor payload as a domain declines here; a fielded
// summand inside an `SEnum` is invisible to this walk by design and declines
// at the rim's field rule (the checker-accepts/rim-declines split).
theorem sig_t0_fielded_payload_domain_declines():
    sig_pi(SFlds, SNat) = SNo
{ refl }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The shells: the only part of this file that is layer m.
//
// One elimination over code each — the transcriber over the marked body, the
// signature checker over the fn's type — arms that are calls, and a default.
// Everything they do is call the helpers above, which is the point — the
// analysis is ordinary Heather, and the modal layer contributes the traversal
// and nothing else (design doc §4).
//
// Why the arms are what they are:
//
//  * the motive is `TA`, a **context-free** type. It has to be: `tm_lam`'s and
//    `tm_elim_nat`'s recursive results live at *extended* contexts, and a
//    motive mentioning the context would put them at `Code((g, x) |- …)` with
//    no way back down to `g`. One constant motive is also what makes 46
//    unwritten branches cost one `_` arm.
//  * `tm_app` recognizes a marker — the identity lambda applied to a
//    numeral — or assembles a call onto a callee chain (convention 3). No
//    other application exists in marked fragment code: a raw global
//    reference would go neutral (`elim^Trm` on `box (u^δ)`), which is
//    exactly why the reflection primitive replaces call heads with markers
//    before boxing.
//  * `tm_var` answers `TVarLeaf` and nothing else. A variable's *identity* is
//    unobservable at layer m (the recursor's variable branch abstracts over it
//    at layer v), which is exactly why occurrences arrive pre-marked.
//  * the arms are named for `heather_kernel::rec::BRANCH_NAMES`, read at
//    elaboration rather than restated here.
//
// Everything not listed declines, and declining is an answer: the generator is
// untrusted, so a body this walker cannot read is a function that does not get
// a `Compile` instance, never a wrong theorem.
meta fn transcribe(g: Ctx, arity: Nat, body: Code(g |- Nat))
    for certgen.transcribe → TOut
{
    ta_finish(arity, match code body as TA {
        tm_zero                   => ta_zero(),
        tm_succ(t)                => ta_succ(t),
        tm_var                    => ta_var(),
        tm_lam(dom, b)            => ta_lam(dom, b),
        tm_app(dom, cod, hd, arg) => ta_app(arity, hd, arg),
        tm_elim_nat(m, z, s, sc)  => ta_elim(arity, z, s, sc),
        // Convention 5: the tier-1 eliminator and its constructor case tree.
        // The shape/arity/motive results are bound and never consulted (they
        // carry global references the walk must not force); everything the
        // transcription needs arrives through the markers.
        tm_case(a, b, m, l, r, sc) => ta_case(l, r, sc),
        tm_elim_wf(sh, n, m, st, sc) => ta_elim_wf(arity, st, sc),
        // Convention 4: the tier-0 formers a non-`Nat` result type's
        // constructors are built from. The type arguments of each are bound
        // and ignored — this shell analyses terms, and a constructor's
        // summand annotations say nothing its index does not.
        tm_triv                   => ta_triv(),
        tm_pair(fty, sty, a, b)   => ta_pair(a, b),
        tm_inl(lty, rty, v)       => ta_inl(v),
        tm_inr(lty, rty, v)       => ta_inr(v),
        _                         => ta_stuck(),
    })
}

// The signature checker's shell: the same `match code`, over a TYPE code —
// the fn's signature, pulled by the elaborator's reflection primitive
// (docs/fn-signature-reflection-design.md §1) — eliminating with the
// recursor's `ty_*` side. `ty_pi` binds its two recursive results (the
// domain's verdict and the codomain's; the codomain's is computed at an
// extended local context, which is why the motive must be context-free here
// exactly as it is above). Every other type former — Σ, W, a user
// inductive's inlined lowering, a dependent codomain that is not a
// `Nat`-chain — takes the default arm and declines.
meta fn check_sig(g: Ctx, sig: TyCode(g))
    for certgen.check_sig → SigOut
{
    sig_finish(match code sig as SA {
        ty_nat           => SNat,
        ty_unit          => SPure,
        ty_pi(ds, dt)    => sig_pi(ds, dt),
        ty_sigma(ss, st) => sig_sigma(ss, st),
        ty_sum(sl, sr)   => sig_sum(sl, sr),
        // A tier-1 parameter type: the shape's walk is the verdict, and the
        // arity term's recursive result is bound and never forced — it may
        // contain global references the walk must not meet
        // (docs/certgen-tier1-source-types-design.md §5).
        ty_wf(ws, wn)    => sig_wf(ws),
        _                => SNo,
    })
}
